			<div class="content" id="post-content-4236384">
									

<p>Thank you so much for your crit! UPDATE: Grammar and formatting errors corrected!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;Block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Continuing to feel like a cop-out. How did they look inside if all of the above was true? I would pick one deterrent and stick with it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I've decided to stick with the embarrassment, coupled with recording failure. Yes, it's a cop-out, but like you said, I'm struggling to find an alternative. In the least, the embarrassment angle feels like a simple amplification, and a play on the absurdity of SCP-4281. After all, you're literally trying to force your way into/peek inside a bathroom stall.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This whole log was fucking amazing, and I loved every minute of it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Cheers. Dialogue is definitely easier for me than technical writing. I'm still getting the hang of the &quot;clinical&quot; tone of Articles.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This one wasn't as funny as the first one, but I can't see a version of this that doesn't dash your pacing to the rocks without including it. Maybe trim it down slightly?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I had some trouble with this one for sure. I wanted it to be quick and to the point, a good lull between Log 1 and Log 13. Just establish that this person, this thing, isn't looking to escape, we have nothing to offer it, and honestly, it just wants to ramble.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Okay woah, I did not see this coming, and holy shit was it good. I would honestly have upvoted if this was just the first log, not gonna lie. Some crazy cowboy in a bathroom stall that doesn't shut up. But then this hit, and goddamn.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>With my previous compliment, I do have a caveat. I'm not a fan of this as the ending. The whole way through that interview, I was thinking that -1 was getting all personal and nasty due to their attempt at being fake to him (lying, etc.), and then this final bit here clashes with that change in temper in a way that I'm not sure I like. I honestly think it'd be better if you ended at &quot;So I said to him- Oh! Uh, well, alright then! Have a good night fellas! Come back any time!&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, here's the funny part! If you check for &quot;Stalled Conversation&quot; on the Drafts forum, you'll see my original draft was very much in-line with what you were hoping. Originally, there was only one test log, a variation on 1-13, that portrayed SCP-4281-1 as more malicious. It closed with &quot;Y'all come back any time!&quot;</p>
<p>Along with my official crit, no less than three people in chat said they disliked SCP-4281-1 as spiteful or manipulative, and found the farcically tragic angle more compelling. Additionally, Lev (thanks again!) thought the narrative pacing was too quick-to-the-punch with only one test log.</p>
<p>So, eh! What could have been. It's one of those &quot;you can't please everyone&quot; situations, but I'm happy with the ending, and the extended logs.</p>
<p>Y'all have a good day now!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><sub>Please collapse long posts ~ Leveritas</sub></p>

