<p>This is great.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;long&nbsp;rant</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;long&nbsp;rant</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The image is an amazing hook. You're just invited to imagine yourself entering a nondescript bathroom, and then being pulled into a conversation with an unknown figure sitting in the stall.</p>
<p>The dialogue really takes the typical &quot;SCP with interview log&quot; and just throws it out the window. He's not the meek interviewee who just answers plain questions directly, and he's not the monologuer who divulges a long story about the subject at hand. Instead, the poor interviewer is being dragged along like they're tied to the back of a truck going down the highway, trying desperately to turn the conversation to something useful as the guy just goes on and on and on.</p>
<p>The last interview further exemplified this, with a reference to somebody the interviewer cared about, but was let completely unsatisfied as he started rambling about a K-mart. You finish that incident with a statement by the guard, using a redaction to spark the imagination about whether he was threatened, told the bad news, or something else entirely.</p>
<p>And then finally the ending, when the scip realizes he may have a problem.</p>
<p>You can even look at this as metaphorical in a way: the scip is stuck in a bathroom, and like in life, can't really control what people he comes across. Deep down he wants to be understood and connect with other people, but his self-imposed separation prevents that. He both is physically and emotionally isolated, and he doesn't know how to pull himself out.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Well done author. +1</p>

							</div>

								
