			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4240372">
									

<p>I'm guessing you replied to the wrong comment (pardon in advance if that's not the case!), so:</p>
<p>There's a lot of weird, simple solutions you can use here. One of the things to keep in mind is that your solution doesn't have to plug every &quot;hole&quot;; it just has to be interesting, brief, and fun enough for us to (mostly) not care. For example:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>Describe any attempt to approach the stall from above or otherwise intrude in its space as suffering from <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeno%27s_paradoxes">Zeno's dichotomy paradox</a>. You can only cover half the distance with each attempt (both people and machines perpetually stop half way, thinking they've arrived, only to &quot;realize&quot; they've only traveled half the distance).</li>
<li>Just make him a cognitohazard that people can't describe (even when looking at a picture). If you want to elaborate on this, refer to <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_vision">visual recognition bots</a> describing him as something silly (&quot;duck with squirrel on head&quot;).</li>
<li>Have every attempt to look into his stall result in person or machine looking in the wrong stall. Make how this works vague and confusing (implying the Foundation doesn't understand it themselves).</li>
<li>Everyone and everything that looks inside the stall proceeds to spontaneuosly combust. Make a brief humorous aside/implication about the Foundation discovering that this includes things literally incapable of combusting.</li>
<li>The stall is literally empty. You can only see his feet from underneath it; you're &quot;peeking into&quot; a completely different space. Attempts to explore this space via endoscope or crawling through result in you entering another stall (or even better, you just &quot;come out&quot; the same way you came in). Again, you can keep this vague and confusing.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I'm fond of the Xeno solution, if only because I think it emphasizes the futility of the skip (reflected in their inability to get useful information, and his inability to make a human connection). I'd skip the drones and just mention the problem occurs with attempts at endoscopic exploration (because endoscopic exploration of a bathroom stall is both incredibly pervy but also very logical and something the Foundation would probably do).</p>

							</div>

								
