<p>Thanks! I owe the subversion concept to my critters, honestly. They gave me the idea. My original concept was purely malicious, came off as very bland, and lacked a strong ending hook.</p>
<p>As for the &quot;EXPUNGED vs REDACTED&quot; issue, I did try to remain consistent with Foundation lore when using it. According to: <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/forum/t-229758/expunged-versus-redacted">http://www.scp-wiki.net/forum/t-229758/expunged-versus-redacted</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;[DATA EXPUNGED]&quot; when (in-universe) removing classified data — stuff that the reader doesn't have clearance to see. &quot;[DATA REDACTED]&quot; will just mean that something has been removed for clarity or appropriateness of expression&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Which seemed fairly logical to me. What the security officer said would have been deliberately erased by the Foundation archives because of any number of sinister reasons, left ambiguous.</p>
<p>If you, or anyone else has any ideas for means by which SCP-4281-1 remains (mostly) imperceptible, I'd love to hear it! I definitely struggled with finding a credible means of giving SCP-4281-1 it's characteristic &quot;feet, socks and shoes&quot; appearance.</p>

